# Cloudflare Pages reads this from the OUTPUT directory, which for this project
# is the site root itself (output `.`, root directory `sailing`). It is not served.
#
# Rule order matters: Pages applies every matching rule, and a later one wins on
# a repeated header name. Keep the broad rule first and the narrow ones after.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

# Everything under assets/ is cached for a year. `immutable` tells the browser
# not to revalidate at all - that is the half that actually removes the request,
# and it is also why a file replaced under its own name reaches nobody who
# already has it. Cloudflare's edge kept a replaced gallery.js through a deploy
# (#95), and a purge would not have reached a browser that held it.
#
# So a replaced file gets a new URL, and how depends on what it is:
#   - assets/shared/css/ and assets/shared/js/ (copied from shared/) keep their
#     filenames. Every page names them with ?v=<the first 10 hex digits of the
#     file's sha256>. After editing one, run `python tools/assetver.py`, which
#     rewrites every reference. The gate (tools/linkcheck.py) refuses a page
#     whose version does not match the file, and tools/photos.py stamps every
#     trip log it renders.
#   - fonts, images and trip-log photos are not versioned: rename rather than
#     overwrite. A font is named both in each page's preload and in a url() in
#     tokens.css, and the two must match exactly or it is fetched twice. The
#     trip-log derivatives are written once per photo and never edited in
#     place; a photo re-encoded under the same filename would be wrong for a
#     year.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The HTML itself must not be cached for a year, or a deploy would not reach
# anyone who had already visited. This is the rule the one above would otherwise
# swallow if a page ever lived under assets/.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
